2026-09-30 21:23:52,778 src.graph.nodes INFO Agent input: {'messages': [HumanMessage(content='# Research Topic\n\nComprehensive Comparison of Vector Databases for RAG Workloads: Query Latency, Scalability, and Operational Cost\n\n# Completed Research Steps\n\n## Completed Step 1: Retrieve Benchmark Data on Query Latency (p99), Throughput, and Recall at Scale\n\n<finding>\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "crawl_tool", "arguments": {"url":"https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026"}}\n</tool_call>\n</finding>\n\n## Completed Step 2: Analyze Scalability and Architecture for RAG Workloads\n\n<finding>\n# Comprehensive Comparison of Vector Databases for RAG Workloads: Query Latency, Scalability, and Operational Cost\n\n## Problem Statement  \nAnalyze the architectural design, indexing strategies, scalability characteristics, and operational performance of vector databases—specifically Milvus, Qdrant, Weaviate, and pgvector—in the context of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) workloads. Focus on how these databases handle large-scale vector retrieval, memory usage, partitioning, distributed deployment, hybrid search, similarity search, and real-time indexing.\n\n---\n\n## Research Findings\n\n### 1. Milvus: Scalable Architecture with Distributed Indexing  \nMilvus is designed with a highly scalable architecture that supports distributed vector storage and indexing, making it suitable for large-scale RAG applications. It uses a multi-dimensional indexing strategy that includes both approximate and exact search capabilities. Milvus supports dynamic partitioning of vector data across nodes, enabling efficient load balancing and reducing query latency during high-traffic RAG scenarios. Its architecture allows for horizontal scaling via a distributed storage layer and supports real-time indexing with low-latency updates. The database is optimized for high-throughput similarity searches, which is critical for RAG pipelines that require fast retrieval of relevant context. Milvus also supports hybrid search, combining vector and keyword queries to improve retrieval accuracy in complex RAG workflows.\n\n> Source: [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Qdrant: Lightweight and Efficient for Real-Time RAG  \nQdrant is built with a focus on performance and low-latency operations, making it ideal for real-time RAG applications. Its architecture emphasizes minimal memory overhead and fast query execution, which is essential for latency-sensitive RAG systems. Qdrant uses a compact indexing structure that supports efficient similarity search and real-time indexing without requiring extensive infrastructure. It enables dynamic partitioning of data based on user-defined criteria and supports distributed deployment through a scalable cluster model. While not as optimized for massive-scale deployments as Milvus, Qdrant excels in small to mid-sized RAG systems where low latency and minimal resource consumption are priorities. The database also supports hybrid search, allowing RAG pipelines to combine vector and text-based retrieval for richer context.\n\n> Source: [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaviate: Full-Text and Vector Integration with Scalable Search  \nWeaviate is designed to support hybrid search, combining vector-based similarity search with full-text keyword queries. This makes it particularly effective for RAG applications that require both semantic and keyword-based retrieval. The database uses a graph-based architecture with a scalable indexing system that allows for efficient partitioning and distributed query processing. Weaviate supports real-time indexing and can scale across multiple nodes, enabling large-scale deployment in enterprise RAG environments. Its architecture includes built-in support for schema-based data modeling, which allows users to define custom data types and relationships. This flexibility enhances retrieval accuracy in complex RAG pipelines. However, memory usage can increase with larger datasets due to the overhead of metadata indexing.\n\n> Source: [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)\n\n---\n\n### 4. pgvector: Embedded Database with Limited Scalability  \npgvector is an extension of PostgreSQL that enables vector operations directly within the relational database. It is best suited for small to medium-sized RAG applications where integration with existing SQL systems is a priority. While pgvector supports similarity search and real-time indexing, its scalability is limited due to the inherent constraints of a single-node relational database architecture. It lacks native support for distributed partitioning and does not natively support large-scale vector retrieval. Memory usage increases significantly with dataset size, and performance degrades under high query loads. pgvector is not designed for distributed deployments and is less efficient in handling large-scale RAG workloads compared to specialized vector databases.\n\n> Source: [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion  \nIn the context of RAG workloads, the scalability and architectural design of vector databases vary significantly:\n\n- **Milvus** offers the strongest scalability and distributed indexing, ideal for enterprise-grade, high-throughput RAG systems.\n- **Qdrant** provides excellent real-time performance and low memory overhead, making it suitable for lightweight, latency-sensitive RAG applications.\n- **Weaviate** excels in hybrid search capabilities and supports rich schema modeling, enhancing retrieval accuracy in complex RAG pipelines.\n- **pgvector** is best used in small-scale or integrated environments where SQL compatibility is essential, but it lacks the scalability and distributed features required for large-scale RAG deployments.\n\nFor production RAG systems requiring high scalability, low latency, and real-time indexing, Milvus and Qdrant are recommended. For applications needing hybrid search and schema flexibility, Weaviate is a strong choice. pgvector remains a viable option only for niche, small-scale use cases.\n\n---\n\n## References  \n- [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)  \n\n- [Vector Database Benchmark 2026 – Ailog](https://app.ailog.fr/en/blog/guides/vector-database-benchmark-2026)\n</finding>\n\n# Current Step\n\n## Title\n\nSynthesize and Compare Performance, Cost, and Operational Readiness Across Databases\n\n## Description\n\nCompare the findings from the research steps to produce a structured, side-by-side analysis of query latency, scalability, and operational cost (including self-hosted vs cloud, per-GB cost, maintenance overhead) for each database. Evaluate reliability of sources, identify gaps, and assess which databases are best suited for production RAG systems with emphasis on real-world operational readiness (HA, multi-tenancy, backup, Kubernetes).\n\n## Locale\n\nen-US', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={})]}
